## Install

Every route ends with `whisper-dictate-local --setup`, which looks at your
hardware, recommends a model, downloads it and the speech engine, and offers
to bind a key. Nothing here needs PyPI — both files are attached to this
release.

### Debian, Ubuntu, Mint

```bash
curl -LO @REPO@/releases/download/v@VERSION@/whisper-dictate-local_@VERSION@_all.deb
sudo apt install ./whisper-dictate-local_@VERSION@_all.deb
whisper-dictate-local --setup
```

apt resolves GTK, the app indicator, Keybinder, `xdotool`, `xclip` and
PulseAudio for you. The leading `./` matters — without a slash, apt reads the
argument as a package name to look up.

### pip or pipx — any other Unix

From the wheel attached to this release:

```bash
pipx install @REPO@/releases/download/v@VERSION@/whisper_dictate_local-@VERSION@-py3-none-any.whl
whisper-dictate-local --setup
```

Or straight from the repository, for unreleased changes:

```bash
pipx install git+@REPO@
```

Or with the macOS menu bar front end (rumps and PyObjC):

```bash
pip install 'whisper-dictate-local[macos] @ git+@REPO@'
```

`pip install` works in place of `pipx install` everywhere above; pipx is
suggested only because it keeps the app in its own environment.

`requests` is the only dependency pulled in — everything else is an external
command probed for at run time, so there is nothing to compile. **The GTK
tray is deliberately not a Python dependency**: PyGObject and the app
indicator typelibs cannot be installed reliably from PyPI, so they come from
your system package manager. The command-line half works without them, and
`whisper-dictate-local-tray` prints the package names if they are missing.

### From a checkout

```bash
git clone @REPO@
cd whisper-dictate-local
./install.sh          # desktop entry, icons, launchers
whisper-dictate-local --setup
```

`./tools/make_deb.sh` builds the `.deb` from the same checkout.
